let's talk to the uh foreign secretary  dominic raab who  joins us now look good to see you this  morning is this a definite now do we  need to have a yay or an a by sunday  i think we probably do you never rule  anything out in  talks with the eu the prime minister is  shown he  wants to leave no stone unturned in the  search for a deal that's why he was out  in brussels  uh speaking to ursula van der leyn he's  also  made clear and i think both sides  recognize there are still significant  differences  between them we'll continue with the  negotiations this week i think they'll  take a stop take and decide  the future the negotiations on sunday  and  the the point from the uk side is as i  said we'll leave no stone unturned we  would like a free trade deal with the eu  but we're not going to sacrifice  the basic points of democratic principle  on fisheries on  control over our laws as we leave the  transition period now  i think it's important that that's  recognized  on the eu side and if they do i think  the scope for a deal is still there to  be done  again it's only last year that the prime  minister said that  uh it would be a failure of state craft  if we left with no deal when it comes to  trade i mean  why has it got this far if that's his  view  well we obviously have left with a deal  in terms of the draw agreement and north  valley protocol but in relation to the  fda  frankly we just haven't seen enough  pragmatism and flexibility on the eu  side and it's worth just reminding  um people at home what we're talking  about here we're talking about two  key points there's a range of issues but  two key points  the concept that the uk would leave the  transition period  uh as an independent coastal state but  without control of our fisheries  that's something that no uh country in  the world  would uh that has accepted is in the  position  of why would the uk and likewise  when we leave we should be in control of  our laws we'll accept the  kind of requirements in the eu's own  free trade agreements whether it's korea  um or canada often talked about we're  not going to do is allow the eu  undemocratically to control our laws in  this country and i i think those are  two basic points of principle that no  other country around the world would  um accept in in dealing with the e or  anyone else as an independent state and  i think it's important that the eu um  with it will  recognize that if it does a free trade  deal is still there to be done if it  won't i think on sunday  we take stock and uh we we take a  decision and both sides recognize we  need some finality  on where we are i mean where does that  leave  the uk in terms of negotiation i mean  you know we can make as many demands as  we like it doesn't mean they're going to  be met we've got to be  prepared to compromise on something  haven't we  yes exactly and that's why we got to the  position we have of saying  okay we'll look at the deals that you've  done with canada  um that something like that would seem  to be about right  um and uh we've been flexible on the  issue of fisheries we recognize  uh the need for to transition but what  we're not going to do  is uh indefinitely allow the eu to  control our fisheries or our laws  and i think most people would recognize  that and countries around the world  wouldn't accept those terms  the eu wouldn't accept those terms  dealing with anyone else so  um those are the points of democratic  principle if the eu accepts them  there's a deal to be done but i think uh  given the bridge  the gaps that remain we'll keep going at  it you know that's why the pm was out in  brussels he wants to get a deal  we're doing everything we can i think we  uh uh  behave reasonably and flexibly and  pragmatically throughout  um and if the eu reciprocate that  there's still a deal to be done  if it doesn't we'll take stock we'll do  that on sunday and we'll be prepared to  leave on australia-style terms  and make a success of brexit but there  is going to be some fallout as  i mean as we look at that being  certainly a very  realistic possibility at this stage i  mean there are reports in the papers  this morning that come the first of  january  people from the uk may not be able to  just  travel in europe as freely as they they  have done partly because of the covid  restrictions what have you but no  exemptions for the uk and this could  have  sort of very strong impacts on the  british people very quickly couldn't it  well if it's right to say i mean in any  event there'll be changes come january  with a free trade deal or even if we  leave on australia style terms that's  certainly true  um there'll be uh some froth around  those changes but i think we're well  placed with the  contingency planning that we're doing to  brace those and make a success of brexit  um you mentioned travelling the eu of  course the eu is already we've already  agreed  that uh in any case that there will be  um uh the uk travelers can  can go on visa waiver to the eu for uh i  believe it's 90 out of 100  days at a time so in terms of the usual  course of  uh travel um there's already  arrangements that will deal with that  but you're right to say that it it may  be bumpy if we don't get a free trade  deal  on the other hand there are positive  signs this trust has amassed a whole  range of free trade deals  not just with existing eu partners um or  indeed other partners that have  done deals with the eu but there for  example the japanese  uh free trade deal goes beyond the eu  president with data  and digital um we know that from merck  the pharmaceutical company to netflix  uh just recently in the matter of last  few weeks that both of those  have announced plans for new hubs in  london because they see from pharma to  creative uh industries the uk is a world  leader  so i i do think there will be some  changes i do think it could be bumpy in  certain areas but also huge  opportunities  i don't think we should lose sight of  that we're going the extra mile we're  leaving no stone unturned  to try and get an fta with the eu but  they've got to be reasonable about those  points of principle as i said look i'm  sure just about everyone uh  watching this at home have seen the  adverts  that the government's put out for for  businesses small and large alike saying  you know changes are coming you need to  be prepared for the changes  i mean the problem for business would  seem to be they don't know what those  changes  are how can anyone prepare for something  that's going to happen in three weeks  and we don't know what it is  well that's not quite right there some  of the um changes will come  on either eventuality um and what we've  been doing is communicating  uh all the likely requirements and what  the difference between  having an fda and leaving on australian  style terms would be but you're right to  say and i certainly said  we need some point of finality to give  them certainty so that they can  know which set of rules and which set of  um  uh uh approaches and declarations that  they need to  to adhere to that's why sunday i think  is an important moment  you never say never in these talks but i  think we do need to get some finality  for the reasons that you've given  um i think we'll get that on sunday i  think both sides recognize it's time for  that  just a final thought on this uh you  think i mean it  is sunday a hard deadline or not  i think we view it as a point uh where  we need some finality  i'm just a bit reticent uh ever to say  you know you can never say never with  these eu negotiations and of course it  depends  uh if the eu moves if the eu moves  substantially  and actually we're only dotting a few  eyes or crossing a few t's  might be different but i think without  movement on the crucial two  three areas that i've described um i  think that that will be a point of  finality  um and that's certainly the way that the  uk side is approaching it  um 